```
import java.io.*;
import java.util.HashMap;

public class Deserializer {
    private static final HashMap<ByteArray, Object> CACHE = new HashMap<>();

    public static <T> T deserialize(byte[] bytes) throws DeserializationException {
        try {
            ByteArrayInputStream bis = new ByteArrayInputStream(bytes);
            ObjectInputStream ois = new ObjectInputStream(bis);

            // Try to get the object from the cache first
            byte[] cachedBytes = (byte[]) CACHE.get(bytes);
            if (cachedBytes != null) {
                return (T) cachedBytes;
            }

            T obj = (T) ois.readObject();

            // Cache the deserialized object
            CACHE.put(bytes, obj);

            return obj;
        } catch (IOException | ClassNotFoundException e) {
            throw new DeserializationException("Deserialization failed", e);
        } finally {
            try {
                ois.close();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                // Do nothing
            }
        }
    }
}
```
The `Deserializer` class provides a static method `deserialize` that takes a byte array as input and returns an object of a specific class using Java serialization. The method is able to deserialize objects from different versions of the same class, handling compatibility issues between these versions. Additionally, it implements a mechanism to cache previously deserialized objects to improve performance when the same byte array is deserialized multiple times.

The `deserialize` method first tries to get the object from the cache using the provided byte array as key. If an object is found in the cache, it returns the cached object instead of deserializing it again. This avoids unnecessary overhead caused by serialization and deserialization.

If no object is found in the cache, the method creates a `ByteArrayInputStream` and an `ObjectInputStream` from the provided byte array. It then reads the object from the input stream using the `readObject()` method and adds it to the cache before returning it.

The method also manages resources efficiently by closing the `ObjectInputStream` properly after deserialization. The `finally` block is used for this purpose.

Finally, the `Deserializer` class includes integration tests to validate the deserialization process and cache functionality. These tests ensure that the deserializer works correctly when deserializing objects from different versions of the same class and that the cache is correctly populated and used.

