This example shows how we can use pthreads in C to create a thread pool that can be used to perform concurrent HTTP requests. We create a fixed number of worker threads at the start of our program, and then use a queue to submit tasks for execution. Each worker thread pulls the next task off the queue, performs it, and then goes back to sleep until there are more tasks available.

The `queue` is implemented using a linked list of `struct request` objects. The `struct request` object has two fields: `url` which is a string representing the URL of the HTTP request, and `done` which is a boolean flag indicating whether the request has been completed or not. When we add a new task to the queue, we set `done` to false and then signal the condition variable `cv` using the `pthread_cond_signal()` function. The worker thread will then wake up and pull the next task off the queue, process it, and then go back to sleep until there are more tasks available.

