Here is an example of how to implement a thread pool using pthreads in C:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>
#include <unistd.h>

// Define the structure for the requests and the queue
struct request {
    char *url;
    int done;
};
typedef struct request request_t;
request_t* queue = NULL;

// Function to add a request to the queue
void addRequest(char *url) {
    // Create a new request structure
    request_t *newRequest = malloc(sizeof(request_t));
    newRequest->url = url;
    newRequest->done = false;

    // Add the request to the end of the queue
    if (queue == NULL) {
        queue = newRequest;
    } else {
        request_t *temp = queue;
        while (temp->next != NULL) {
            temp = temp->next;
        }
        temp->next = newRequest;
    }
}

// Function to process a request
void* processRequest(void* args) {
    // Get the URL for the request from the argument
    char *url = (char*)args;

    // Perform the HTTP request and get the response
    FILE *fp = fopen(url, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to open file %s\n", url);
        return;
    }
    char buffer[1024];
    while (fgets(buffer, 1024, fp) != NULL) {
        // Do something with the response data
        printf("%s", buffer);
    }
    fclose(fp);

    // Mark the request as complete and signal the condition variable
    request_t *request = (request_t*)args;
    request->done = true;
    pthread_cond_signal(&cv);
}

// Function to wait for a request to be processed
void waitForRequest(char *url) {
    // Add the request to the queue and signal the condition variable
    addRequest(url);
    pthread_cond_signal(&cv);

    // Wait for the request to be completed
    while (1) {
        request_t *request = (request_t*)url;
        if (request->done == true) {
            break;
        }
        usleep(100000); // 100 ms
    }
}

int main() {
    // Create a fixed number of worker threads
    int nThreads = 5;
    pthread_t *threads = malloc(nThreads * sizeof(pthread_t));

    for (int i = 0; i < nThreads; i++) {
        // Create a new thread and start it running
        pthread_create(&threads[i], NULL, processRequest, (void*)url);
    }

    // Wait for the threads to finish
    for (int i = 0; i < nThreads; i++) {
        pthread_join(threads[i], NULL);
    }

    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we define a `struct request` that contains the URL for the HTTP request and a boolean flag to indicate whether the request has been completed. We also define a global variable `queue` to store the requests in a linked list.

We have three functions: `addRequest`, `processRequest`, and `waitForRequest`. The `addRequest` function takes a URL as an argument and adds it to the end of the `queue` linked list. It also signals the condition variable `cv` to wake up any threads waiting for requests to be processed.

The `processRequest` function is called by each worker thread in the pool. It takes the URL of the request as an argument, opens the file corresponding to the URL using `fopen`, reads the response data from the file using `fgets`, and then marks the request as complete by setting the `done` flag to `true`. It also signals the condition variable `cv` to wake up any threads waiting for the request to be processed.

The `waitForRequest` function takes a URL as an argument and waits until the request corresponding to that URL is marked as complete. It uses `pthread_cond_signal()` to signal the condition variable `cv` whenever a new request is added to the queue, so it can wake up any threads waiting for requests to be processed.

In the main function, we create a fixed number of worker threads using `pthread_create`. Each thread calls `processRequest` with its own URL as an argument. We then wait for all of the worker threads to finish using `pthread_join`.

Note that this is just one example of how you might implement a thread pool using pthreads in C. There are many other ways to do it, and the specific implementation will depend on your requirements.

