    }
  }

  // Function to pause the data collection process
  function pause() {
    paused = true;
  }

  // Function to resume the data collection process
  function resume() {
    if (paused) {
      saveData();
      paused = false;
    }
  }

  // Function to handle user input for pausing and resuming
  function handleUserInput(input) {
    switch (input) {
      case "pause":
        pause();
        break;
      case "resume":
        resume();
        break;
      default:
        console.log(`Invalid input: ${input}`);
        break;
    }
  }

  // Add event listeners for user input and stream data
  const userInput = document.getElementById("user-input");
  const streamData = document.getElementById("stream-data");

  userInput.addEventListener("click", function(event) {
    handleUserInput(event.target.value);
  });

  streamData.addEventListener("data", function(event) {
    addToBuffer(event.data);
  });

  // Start collecting data from the stream
  const reader = new ReadableStream({
    start() {
      console.log("Starting to read from the stream");
    },
    pull(controller) {
      controller.enqueue(streamData.read());
    }
  });

  reader.pipeTo(new WritableStream({
    write(data) {
      addToBuffer(data);
    }
  }));
}
```
In this code, we define a function called `collectDataFromStream` that takes two arguments: a stream and a database. The function sets up a buffer to store data read from the stream and adds an event listener to handle user input for pausing and resuming the data collection process. It also defines functions for adding data to the buffer, saving data to the database, and handling user input.

The `addToBuffer` function is called when new data is read from the stream and adds it to the buffer. If the buffer reaches a certain size (defined by the `batchSize` variable), the function saves the data to the database using the `saveData` function.

The `saveData` function tries to save the data to the database, catching any errors that may occur during the process. It then clears the buffer and logs a message indicating how many records were saved.

The `pause` and `resume` functions are used to pause or resume the data collection process. When the function is paused, it clears the buffer and sets the `paused` variable to true. When the function is resumed, it saves any remaining data in the buffer and sets the `paused` variable to false.

The `handleUserInput` function is called when a user clicks on a button with an ID of "user-input" or enters input into an element with an ID of "stream-data". It checks the input value and calls the appropriate function (either `pause` or `resume`) depending on the input.

The function then starts reading from the stream using a `ReadableStream` object, piping it to a `WritableStream` object that calls the `addToBuffer` function for each chunk of data read from the stream. This allows us to handle the data as it is read from the stream in real-time.

Finally, the function sets up event listeners for user input and stream data, allowing the user to pause or resume the data collection process as needed.

