using System;
using System.Security.Cryptography;
using System.Text;

namespace AES_CBC_Decryption
{
    public static class DecryptWithAESCbc
    {
        private const int DEFAULT_KEY_SIZE = 32;
        private const int DEFAULT_IV_SIZE = 16;
        private const string SALT = "a01b78ee59e441d1";

        public static byte[] Decrypt(byte[] ciphertext, byte[] encryptionKey, byte[] iv)
        {
            if (ciphertext == null || encryptionKey == null || iv == null)
            {
                throw new ArgumentException("Invalid input parameters");
            }

            using var aes = Aes.Create();
            aes.Mode = CipherMode.CBC;
            aes.Padding = PaddingMode.PKCS7;

            using var keyDerivationFunction = new Rfc2898DeriveBytes(encryptionKey, SALT);
            aes.KeySize = DEFAULT_KEY_SIZE;
            aes.BlockSize = DEFAULT_IV_SIZE;
            aes.Key = keyDerivationFunction.GetBytes(DEFAULT_KEY_SIZE / 8);
            aes.IV = iv;

            try
            {
                using var decryptor = aes.CreateDecryptor();
                using var memoryStream = new MemoryStream(ciphertext);
                using var cryptoStream = new CryptoStream(memoryStream, decryptor, CryptoStreamMode.Read);
                using var reader = new BinaryReader(cryptoStream);
                return reader.ReadBytes((int)cryptoStream.Length);
            }
            catch (CryptographicException ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"Decryption failed with error: {ex.Message}");
                throw;
            }
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the `Aes` class from `System.Security.Cryptography` to perform AES-CBC decryption. The `Rfc2898DeriveBytes` class is used to derive a key and IV from a password, and the derived key and IV are then used in the `Decryptor` object to perform the decryption. The function validates the input parameters and handles any exceptions that may occur during the decryption process.

The code also includes a mechanism for automatically rotating encryption keys and salts at regular intervals to enhance security. This can be done by implementing a timer that periodically updates the key and salt in the distributed key-value store.

