The `start()` method is the entry point for the system, where it subscribes to Kafka topics and starts consuming data in a loop. For each incoming record, it handles the sensor data using the appropriate handler based on the key. If an error occurs during processing, it logs and handles the error.

The `handleSensorData()` method takes a `ConsumerRecord` object as input and decodes the sensor data using the appropriate handler based on the key. It then passes the decoded data to the handler for further processing.

The `getSensorDataHandler()` method retrieves the appropriate handler from the map of sensor data handlers based on the key. If no handler is found, it returns `null`.

The `getTopics()` method returns a list of Kafka topics to subscribe to.

The `sendSummaryAndAlerts()` method sends summarized data and alerts to external monitoring services using an asynchronous messaging protocol. If an error occurs during sending, it logs and handles the error.

The `updateProgress()` method updates the progress of the system in real-time using an external dashboard or monitoring system.

To ensure thread safety and data integrity, we use a concurrent map to store the sensor data handlers. We also use `synchronized` blocks to handle any errors that occur during processing and sending summary and alerts to external monitoring services.

