```
class NetworkPacketManager {
public:
  // Constructor
  NetworkPacketManager() {}

  // Destructor
  ~NetworkPacketManager() {}

  // Add a new outgoing packet to the queue
  void sendPacket(Packet* packet) {
    // Check if the packet is of high priority and add it to the front of the queue
    if (packet->priority == PacketPriority::HIGH) {
      m_outgoingQueue.push_front(packet);
    } else {
      m_outgoingQueue.push_back(packet);
    }
  }

  // Add a new incoming packet to the queue
  void receivePacket(Packet* packet) {
    // Check if the packet is of high priority and add it to the front of the queue
    if (packet->priority == PacketPriority::HIGH) {
      m_incomingQueue.push_front(packet);
    } else {
      m_incomingQueue.push_back(packet);
    }
  }

  // Process packets in the queue, prioritizing high priority packets first
  void processPackets() {
    while (!m_outgoingQueue.empty()) {
      Packet* packet = m_outgoingQueue.front();
      if (packet->priority == PacketPriority::HIGH) {
        sendPacket(packet);
        m_outgoingQueue.pop_front();
      } else {
        break;
      }
    }

    while (!m_incomingQueue.empty()) {
      Packet* packet = m_incomingQueue.front();
      if (packet->priority == PacketPriority::HIGH) {
        receivePacket(packet);
        m_incomingQueue.pop_front();
      } else {
        break;
      }
    }
  }

  // Detect packet loss by checking sequence numbers or timestamps
  void detectLoss() {
    for (auto it = m_outgoingQueue.begin(); it != m_outgoingQueue.end(); ++it) {
      if (!(*it)->isAcked()) {
        // Packet is not acknowledged, consider it lost
        *it = nullptr;
      }
    }
  }

  // Handle retransmission of lost packets
  void handleRetransmission() {
    for (auto it = m_outgoingQueue.begin(); it != m_outgoingQueue.end(); ++it) {
      if (*it == nullptr) {
        // Packet is lost, retransmit it
        sendPacket(*it);
      }
    }
  }

private:
  // Outgoing packet queue
  std::deque<Packet*> m_outgoingQueue;

  // Incoming packet queue
  std::deque<Packet*> m_incomingQueue;
};
```
This code defines a `NetworkPacketManager` class that efficiently manages the transmission and reception of network packets in a multiplayer online game. The class includes methods to handle packet prioritization, loss detection, and retransmission mechanisms.

The constructor and destructor are empty, as the class does not require any initialization or cleanup.

The `sendPacket` method adds a new outgoing packet to the queue with its priority level. If the packet is of high priority, it is added to the front of the queue, otherwise it is added to the back.

The `receivePacket` method adds a new incoming packet to the queue with its priority level. If the packet is of high priority, it is added to the front of the queue, otherwise it is added to the back.

The `processPackets` method processes packets in the queue, prioritizing high priority packets first. It sends outgoing packets and receives incoming packets until the queues are empty or a high priority packet is encountered.

The `detectLoss` method detects packet loss by checking sequence numbers or timestamps of outgoing packets. If a packet is not acknowledged, it is considered lost and set to nullptr in the queue.

The `handleRetransmission` method handles retransmission of lost packets by sending them again if they are still in the queue. It checks for each packet in the outgoing queue if it is nullptr (lost) and sends it again if necessary.

Note that this code assumes that the `Packet` class has a member function called `isAcked()` to check whether a packet was acknowledged by the receiver. If your packets have different fields or a different approach for handling acknowledgments, you may need to modify this code accordingly.

