Here is a sample code for a scalable Node.js server module that efficiently manages the copying of bytes from a list of buffered data chunks received from an external data source:
```javascript
const { EventEmitter } = require('events');
const fs = require('fs');

class DataCopyModule extends EventEmitter {
  constructor(options) {
    super();
    this.options = options;
    this.buffers = []; // List of buffered data chunks received from the external data source
    this.bytesCopied = 0; // Number of bytes copied so far
    this.destination = null; // Destination for the copied bytes (in-memory or disk)
  }

  /**
   * Add a new buffered data chunk to the list of buffers received from the external data source
   * @param {Buffer} chunk A new buffered data chunk received from the external data source
   */
  addChunk(chunk) {
    this.buffers.push(chunk);
    this.bytesCopied += chunk.length;
  }

  /**
   * Start copying the bytes from the list of buffers to the destination buffer or file
   * @param {Buffer|File} destination The destination for the copied bytes (in-memory or disk)
   */
  startCopy(destination) {
    this.destination = destination;
    const copyProcess = async () => {
      while (this.buffers.length > 0) {
        const chunk = this.buffers.shift();
        try {
          await destination.write(chunk);
        } catch (error) {
          // Handle error case: Destination buffer or file is full and cannot accept more data chunks
          if (error instanceof RangeError && error.code === 'ERR_INVALID_OPT_VALUE') {
            this.emit('bufferFull', destination);
          } else {
            throw error;
          }
        }
      }
    };

    copyProcess().then(() => {
      this.emit('copied', this.destination);
    }).catch((error) => {
      this.emit('copyError', error);
    });
  }

  /**
   * Stop copying the bytes and clear the buffers list
   */
  stopCopy() {
    this.buffers = [];
    this.destination = null;
  }
}

module.exports = DataCopyModule;
```
This module extends the `EventEmitter` class to handle events related to the data copy process, such as buffer full or error. It also provides a `startCopy()` method that starts the copying process and an `stopCopy()` method that stops it.

The `addChunk()` method is used to add a new buffered data chunk received from the external data source to the list of buffers. The `startCopy()` method takes in the destination for the copied bytes (in-memory or disk) and starts the copying process by iterating over the buffers list and writing each chunk to the destination buffer or file.

The module also provides a `stopCopy()` method that clears the buffers list and stops the copying process.

This implementation prioritizes maintainability and provides clear, concise documentation for future maintenance and testing. It is designed to be scalable, efficient, and handle potential errors and edge cases gracefully.

