3. We then define a method called `SetTheme(string themeName)`. This method will be responsible for changing the current theme and setting the new theme's properties.
4. Inside the `SetTheme()` method, we first check if the requested theme is available. If it isn't, we throw an exception.
5. Next, we set the `themeBitmap` variable to the bitmap of the requested theme. We use the `MagickImage` class from the ImageMagick library to read the image file and convert it into a MagickImage object.
6. Finally, we call the `OnThemeChanged()` method, which will be responsible for updating the UI based on the new theme.
7. Inside the `OnThemeChanged()` method, we first check if the `themeBitmap` variable is null. If it isn't, we dispose of the previous bitmap.
8. Next, we set the `backgroundImage` property of the form to the new bitmap. We use the `MagickImage` class from the ImageMagick library to read the image file and convert it into a MagickImage object.
9. Finally, we call the `OnThemeChanged()` method on each control that is inside the form. This will ensure that any controls that are created dynamically (e.g. buttons) also get updated with the new theme.
10. We then dispose of the previous bitmap and free up resources by setting the `themeBitmap` variable to null.

That's it! With these few lines of code, we have managed to implement a simple theme manager that can change the background image and color of our form. Of course, this is just the beginning. You can use this as a starting point to build a more complex theme manager that can handle multiple themes, save user preferences, etc.

