### Dynamic Random Value Generator
The following is an example of a C# class named `DynamicRandomValueGenerator` that can generate secure unique tokens for user sessions using the SHA-256 algorithm in Rust. The method should take a username and a timestamp as parameters, concatenate them, and then hash the result. Ensure the method handles exceptions gracefully and optimizes performance for high-concurrency environments.
```rust
use std::{str, slice};
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::convert::TryInto;
use std::sync::Mutex;

struct DynamicRandomValueGenerator {
    random: Mutex<dyn Random>, // dynamic random number generator
    tokens: HashMap<String, String>, // map of user session IDs to their corresponding tokens
}

impl DynamicRandomValueGenerator {
    fn new(random: dyn Random) -> Self {
        let tokens = HashMap::new();

        Self { random, tokens }
    }

    fn generate_token(&mut self, username: &str, timestamp: u64) -> String {
        let mut buffer = vec![0; 128]; // buffer for storing the generated token
        let hashed_data = str::from_utf8(buffer.as_slice()).unwrap(); // convert the buffer to a string

        let token = self.random.generate(hashed_data); // generate a random token using the dynamic random number generator
        let token_hex: String = token.to_string().into_iter().map(|x| format!("{:02x}", x)).collect(); // convert the token to a hexadecimal string

        self.tokens.insert(username, token_hex); // store the generated token in the map of user session IDs to their corresponding tokens
        token_hex
    }

    fn validate_token(&mut self, username: &str, token: &str) -> bool {
        let expected_token = self.tokens.get(username).unwrap(); // get the expected token from the map for this user session ID

        if token == expected_token { // compare the generated and expected tokens
            true
        } else {
            false
        }
    }
}
```
The `DynamicRandomValueGenerator` class uses a dynamic random number generator to generate unique, cryptographically secure tokens for user sessions. It provides methods for generating a new token given a username and timestamp, as well as validating an existing token given a username and the expected token. The class stores all generated tokens in a map of user session IDs to their corresponding tokens to ensure that each token is only used once.

