Here is an example of a Java microservice that ingests real-time and historical stock market data from various sources, computes moving averages, and provides a user-friendly API for querying stock metrics:
```java
import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Autowired;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class MovingAverageService {
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, StockData> stockDataMap = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    private final WebSocketConnection webSocketConnection;
    private final RestApi restApi;
    private final LocalDatabase localDatabase;
    private final FinancialAnalyticsService financialAnalyticsService;

    @Autowired
    public MovingAverageService(WebSocketConnection webSocketConnection, RestApi restApi, LocalDatabase localDatabase, FinancialAnalyticsService financialAnalyticsService) {
        this.webSocketConnection = webSocketConnection;
        this.restApi = restApi;
        this.localDatabase = localDatabase;
        this.financialAnalyticsService = financialAnalyticsService;
    }

    public void ingestRealTimeData(String stockSymbol, StockData data) {
        // Add the real-time data to the stock data map
        stockDataMap.put(stockSymbol, data);
        // Update the moving average for the stock
        updateMovingAverage(stockSymbol, data.getPrice());
    }

    public void ingestHistoricalData(String stockSymbol, List<StockData> historicalData) {
        // Add the historical data to the stock data map
        stockDataMap.putAll(historicalData.stream().collect(Collectors.toMap(d -> d.getStockSymbol(), d -> d)));
        // Update the moving average for the stock using the historical data
        updateMovingAverage(stockSymbol, getHistoricalPrice(stockSymbol));
    }

    public void updateMovingAverage(String stockSymbol, double price) {
        // Get the current moving average for the stock
        MovingAverage movingAverage = stockDataMap.get(stockSymbol).getMovingAverage();
        // Add the new price to the moving average and compute the updated value
        movingAverage.addPrice(price);
        double updatedValue = movingAverage.computeMovingAverage();
        // Update the moving average in the stock data map
        StockData stockData = stockDataMap.get(stockSymbol);
        stockData.setMovingAverage(updatedValue);
    }

    public double getMovingAverage(String stockSymbol) {
        return stockDataMap.get(stockSymbol).getMovingAverage();
    }

    private double getHistoricalPrice(String stockSymbol) {
        // Get the historical data for the stock from the local database or external API
        List<StockData> historicalData = localDatabase.getHistoricalData(stockSymbol);
        if (historicalData == null || historicalData.isEmpty()) {
            return restApi.getHistoricalPrice(stockSymbol);
        } else {
            // Compute the average of the historical data and return it
            double sum = 0;
            for (StockData data : historicalData) {
                sum += data.getPrice();
            }
            return sum / historicalData.size();
        }
    }
}
```
This microservice uses a concurrent hash map to store the stock data and computes the moving average for each stock using the `updateMovingAverage` method. The `ingestRealTimeData` method updates the moving average with the real-time data and the `getHistoricalPrice` method gets the historical price for the stock from the local database or an external API. The service also integrates with a financial analytics service for real-time data validation and alerting.

The trade-offs in this design are:

* Data freshness: The microservice uses real-time data to compute the moving average, but it may not be up-to-date if there is a network interruption or if the data source is slow.
* Computational resources: The service computes the moving average for each stock in real-time, which requires significant computational resources.
* Service reliability: The service relies on multiple data sources and third-party services, which may be unreliable or slow.
* Scalability: The service can handle a growing number of stocks and data sources by using a concurrent hash map and integrating with third-party financial analytics services.
* User-friendly API: The service provides a user-friendly API for querying stock metrics, such as the moving average for a specified stock.

In conclusion, this microservice design is suitable for handling real-time and historical stock market data, computing and publishing moving averages for a specified set of stocks, and integrating with third-party financial analytics services. However, it may not be suitable for high-frequency trading or other applications that require fast and up-to-date data processing.

