use std::time::Instant;
use std::process::Command;
use rustc_driver::{Driver, DriverCli};
use rustc_interface::{interface, InterfaceExt};

fn measure_compilation(program: &str) -> (u64, u64) {
    let start = Instant::now();
    let mut driver = Driver::new(
        program.to_string(),
        vec![],
        DriverCli::default().flag("-Z", "time"),
    );
    let exit_code = driver.run(&mut |_, _| {});
    let end = Instant::now();
    let time = (end - start).as_secs_f64();
    let memory = std::process::Command::new("ps")
        .arg("-o", "rss")
        .arg(program)
        .output()
        .unwrap()
        .stdout;
    (time, memory)
}
```
This function takes a string argument `program` that represents the path to the Rust program you want to compile. It measures the time taken for compiling the program and tracks the resident set size changes during compilation. The function returns a tuple containing the compilation time in seconds (`f64`) and the resident set size (in bytes) of the compiled program.

The `measure_compilation` function uses the `rustc_driver::Driver` struct to run the Rust compiler with the `-Z time` flag, which enables time measurement during compilation. The `DriverCli` struct is used to configure the driver for the compilation process. The `interface` module is used to provide a way to interact with the compiled program from within the driver.

The function runs the driver using the `run` method and passes in a closure that does nothing but returns early if the subprocess fails or exits abnormally. If the subprocess succeeds, the function calculates the compilation time and memory usage by subtracting the start and end times of the process and parsing the output of the `ps` command to get the resident set size of the compiled program.

The `early_error` function is used to report any errors that occur during the compilation process, and the `catch_with_exit_code` function is used to catch any exit codes returned by the subprocess and return them as part of the tuple.

