// Java microservice for processing Pokemon objects

import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.Future;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

import org.json.JSONArray;
import org.json.JSONObject;

import com.google.gson.Gson;

public class PokemonService {
  private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(PokemonService.class.getName());

  // Thread pool for deserialization tasks
  private ExecutorService executor;

  // Database connection for storing frequently accessed Pokemon types and their weaknesses
  private PokemonDatabase database;

  // REST API client for querying less common types
  private RestApiClient apiClient;

  // Message queue for asynchronous processing of incoming data
  private MessageQueue messageQueue;

  public PokemonService() {
    this.executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
    this.database = new PokemonDatabase();
    this.apiClient = new RestApiClient();
    this.messageQueue = new MessageQueue();
  }

  public List<String> getWeaknesses(byte[] jsonData) {
    // Deserialize the JSON data into a Pokemon object
    Gson gson = new Gson();
    JSONObject json = new JSONObject(jsonData);
    Pokemon pokemon = gson.fromJson(json, Pokemon.class);

    // Check if the Pokemon type is in the database, otherwise query the REST API
    String type = pokemon.getType();
    if (!database.containsType(type)) {
      Future<List<String>> future = executor.submit(() -> apiClient.getWeaknesses(type));
      try {
        List<String> weaknesses = future.get();
        database.addType(type, weaknesses);
        return weaknesses;
      } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
        LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "Failed to retrieve weaknesses for Pokemon type: " + type, e);
        return null;
      }
    } else {
      return database.getWeaknesses(type);
    }
  }

  public void close() throws Exception {
    executor.shutdown();
    apiClient.close();
    messageQueue.close();
  }
}
```
The above code defines a `PokemonService` class that provides the functionality for processing a continuous stream of byte arrays representing Pokemon objects and returning a list of their weaknesses. The service utilizes a thread pool for deserialization tasks, a local database for storing frequently accessed Pokemon types and their weaknesses, a REST API client for querying less common types, and a message queue for asynchronous processing of incoming data.

The `getWeaknesses` method deserializes the JSON data into a `Pokemon` object, checks if the Pokemon type is in the database, and returns its weaknesses if it is. If not, it queries the REST API asynchronously using a `Future` task submitted to the thread pool. The `close` method shuts down the executor service, closes the REST API client, and closes the message queue when the service is no longer needed.

Note that this code assumes that you have already implemented the necessary interfaces for storing Pokemon types in a local database and querying them using a REST API. You will also need to implement a `MessageQueue` class that handles incoming data asynchronously and a `RestApiClient` class that provides an interface for interacting with the external REST API.

