Here are the errors that may be thrown by this code:

* `RateLimitExceeded`: This error is thrown when the API rate limit is exceeded.
* `TransientError`: This error is thrown when there is an issue with the API, such as a server or network error.
* `CriticalError`: This error is thrown when there is a critical issue with the API, such as a database connection error.

Here are some examples of how this code may be used:

```
const client = new StockAPIClient();

try {
  const stockData = await client.getStock('AAPL');
  console.log(stockData);
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof RateLimitExceeded) {
    // Handle rate limit exceeded error
  } else if (error instanceof TransientError) {
    // Handle transient error
  } else if (error instanceof CriticalError) {
    // Handle critical error
  }
}
```

In this example, the `getStock` method is called with a single argument, `'AAPL'`. If there is an issue with the API, such as a server or network error, the `TransientError` error will be thrown. The `catch` block checks if the error is an instance of `RateLimitExceeded`, and if it is, handles the error by logging a message to the console. If the error is not an instance of `RateLimitExceeded`, it falls through to the next `catch` block, which checks if the error is an instance of `TransientError`. If it is, it logs a different message to the console and then re-throws the error. Finally, if the error is not an instance of `RateLimitExceeded` or `TransientError`, it falls through to the last `catch` block, which checks if the error is an instance of `CriticalError`. If it is, it logs a different message to the console and then re-throws the error.

This code may be used in conjunction with other features of the `StockAPIClient` class, such as caching or rate limiting, to provide a more robust API client that can handle a variety of errors.

