Write a C++ function `addOrUpdateUser` that takes a `std::forward_list<User>` and two `std::string` parameters: `username` and `email`. The function should search for a user in the forward list based on their `username`. If a user with the given `username` is found, update their email address. If no user with the specified `username` exists, add a new `User` node to the forward list with the provided `username` and `email`.

The `User` struct should contain at least `username` and `email` as its members. Implement a custom comparator for the `std::forward_list` to compare users based on their `username`.

The function should be efficient and handle large lists without significant performance degradation. It should also manage memory resources properly and handle cases where the `username` might not exist in the list. Provide a mechanism to efficiently traverse the forward list without converting it to another type (e.g., vector).

Additionally, implement a check function `testAddOrUpdateUser` to verify the correctness of your solution with various test cases, including edge cases like an empty list and lists with single elements.

```cpp
#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

struct User {
    std::string username;
    std::string email;

    // Custom comparator for comparing users based on their username.
    bool operator==(const User& other) const {
        return username == other.username;
    }
};

// Efficiently adds or updates a user's email in a forward list based on their username.
void addOrUpdateUser(std::forward_list<User>& userList, const std::string& username, const std::string& email);

// Function to test the correctness of the addOrUpdateUser function.
bool testAddOrUpdateUser();
