This implementation uses a separate thread pool to process the PDUs concurrently, with each thread responsible for processing a batch of PDUs. The `processPdus` method is responsible for fetching the next batch of PDUs from the queue and passing them to the thread pool for processing. The `shutdown` method is used to shut down the manager and terminate all threads.

The rate limiter can be implemented using a timer that waits for a certain amount of time between each iteration of the `processPdus` loop. For example, the following code snippet shows how to implement a rate limiter with a delay of 1 second between each iteration:
```c++
#include <chrono>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

class RateLimiter {
private:
    std::chrono::time_point<std::chrono::system_clock> nextIteration;
    const int64_t delayMs;
public:
    explicit RateLimiter(int64_t delayMs) : nextIteration(std::chrono::system_clock::now()), delayMs(delayMs) {}

    void wait() {
        if (nextIteration <= std::chrono::system_clock::now()) {
            nextIteration = std::chrono::system_clock::now() + std::chrono::milliseconds(delayMs);
        } else {
            auto timeToWait = nextIteration - std::chrono::system_clock::now();
            std::this_thread::sleep_for(timeToWait);
        }
    }
};
```
This implementation uses the `std::chrono` library to measure the duration between each iteration of the loop, and sleeps for the remaining time if necessary. The `wait` method is used to wait until it's time for the next iteration.

In order to use the rate limiter in the CRC manager, you can add a reference to an instance of the rate limiter as a member variable, and call its `wait` method before each iteration of the loop in the `processPdus` method. For example:
