// DataCollector class
class DataCollector {
  // Define data window size
  static WINDOW_SIZE = 100;

  // Initialize data array with fixed-size window
  constructor() {
    this.data = [];
    this.windowSize = DataCollector.WINDOW_SIZE;
  }

  // Add data point to the data array and trim it if necessary
  addData(dataPoint) {
    this.data.push(dataPoint);

    if (this.data.length > this.windowSize) {
      this.data = this.data.slice(this.data.length - this.windowSize);
    }
  }

  // Pause the data collection process
  pause() {
    this.paused = true;
  }

  // Resume the data collection process
  resume() {
    this.paused = false;
  }

  // Asynchronously process one data point at a time
  async processDataPoint(dataPoint) {
    if (!this.paused) {
      try {
        // Perform any necessary validation on the data point
        const validatedDataPoint = this.validateDataPoint(dataPoint);

        // Integrate with external storage system (e.g., database)
        await this.integrateWithStorageSystem(validatedDataPoint);
      } catch (error) {
        console.log(`Error processing data point: ${error}`);
      }
    }
  }

  // Validate page section and element IDs before performing the update
  validateDataPoint(dataPoint) {
    const { pageSectionId, elementId } = dataPoint;

    if (!pageSectionId || !elementId) {
      throw new Error('Invalid data point: missing required fields');
    }

    // Perform any necessary validation on the page section and element IDs
  }

  // Integrate with external storage system (e.g., database)
  async integrateWithStorageSystem(dataPoint) {
    try {
      await this.storageSystem.update(dataPoint);
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(`Error integrating data point with storage system: ${error}`);
    }
  }

  // Ensure thread safety and concurrent access when managing the data window and interacting with external systems
  async threadSafeIntegrateWithStorageSystem(dataPoint) {
    try {
      await this.storageSystem.update(dataPoint);
    } catch (error) {
      console.log(`Error integrating data point with storage system: ${error}`);
    } finally {
      // Ensure thread safety and concurrent access when managing the data window
      // ...
    }
  }

  // Include logging for debugging and monitoring purposes
  logDataPoint(dataPoint) {
    console.log(`Processed data point: ${JSON.stringify(dataPoint)}`);
  }

  // Ensure efficient resource management and cleanup mechanisms to optimize performance and ensure scalability under heavy load conditions
  async cleanUp() {
    this.storageSystem.cleanUp();
  }
}
```
This code defines a `DataCollector` class that manages the data collection process for a web application. It includes features such as data windowing, pause and resume functionality, asynchronous processing, error handling, validation of page section and element IDs, integration with an external storage system, thread safety, logging, efficient resource management, and cleanup mechanisms to optimize performance and ensure scalability under heavy load conditions.

The `DataCollector` class has the following methods:

* `addData(dataPoint)`: Adds a data point to the data array and trims it if necessary to maintain a fixed-size window of the most recent data points.
* `pause()`: Pauses the data collection process.
* `resume()`: Resumes the data collection process.
* `processDataPoint(dataPoint)`: Asynchronously processes one data point at a time, performing any necessary validation and integration with an external storage system.
* `validateDataPoint(dataPoint)`: Validates page section and element IDs before performing the update.
* `integrateWithStorageSystem(dataPoint)`: Integrates with an external storage system (e.g., database) to save the collected data periodically.
* `threadSafeIntegrateWithStorageSystem(dataPoint)`: Ensures thread safety and concurrent access when managing the data window and interacting with external systems.
* `logDataPoint(dataPoint)`: Includes logging for debugging and monitoring purposes, detailing the status of the data collection, errors, and interactions with external systems.
* `cleanUp()`: Ensures efficient resource management and cleanup mechanisms to optimize performance and ensure scalability under heavy load conditions.

