This code defines a Java class called `AsyncRequestManager` that is responsible for managing the asynchronous generation and processing of large volumes of HTTP requests in a microservices architecture. The class uses a reactive programming model with backpressure support to handle high throughput and reduce memory usage. It also implements a circuit breaker pattern with customizable failure thresholds and fallback mechanisms to handle service failures gracefully, as well as a distributed tracing mechanism to monitor request flow and latency across the microservices.

The `processRequests` method is the entry point of the class, which takes a list of requests as input and returns a completion stage that completes when all the requests have been processed. The method first retrieves the user preferences, product details, and real-time pricing information for each request using HTTP GET requests to the appropriate microservices. It then generates the dynamic payload data based on the retrieved information, and sends an HTTP POST request to the `microservice-1` with the request and payload data.

