### Endpoint: `/update`

This endpoint will update the data in the pokemon database by reading a JSON file and updating the existing records.

#### HTTP Request

POST /update

* `file`: A JSON file containing an array of pokemons, with each pokemon having three properties: `name`, `type`, and `description`.

#### Response

Returns an HTTP 200 OK response if the update was successful. If any errors occur during the update process, returns an HTTP 500 Internal Server Error response.

### Implementation Strategy

To implement this service, we will use Spring Boot and its web framework to provide a simple RESTful API for accessing and manipulating the data in the pokemon database. We will also use Jackson to parse the JSON file and update the existing records in the database.

Here is an example of how the implementation might look:
```java
@RestController
public class PokemonService {

    @Autowired
    private PokemonRepository repository;

    @PostMapping("/update")
    public ResponseEntity<String> update(@RequestBody String file) throws IOException {
        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        List<Pokemon> pokemons = mapper.readValue(file, new TypeReference<List<Pokemon>>() {});
        repository.saveAll(pokemons);
        return ResponseEntity.ok("Update successful");
    }

    @GetMapping("/pokemons")
    public List<Pokemon> getAllPokemons() {
        return repository.findAll();
    }

    @GetMapping("/pokemons/{name}")
    public Pokemon getPokemonByName(@PathVariable String name) {
        return repository.findByName(name);
    }
}
```
This implementation uses the `@Autowired` annotation to inject a `PokemonRepository` instance into the service, which is responsible for managing the data in the pokemon database. The `update()` method reads a JSON file and updates the existing records in the database using the `saveAll()` method of the repository. The `getAllPokemons()` and `getPokemonByName()` methods return the appropriate data from the repository based on the HTTP request.

